//! Explanations shown under the options of each entry of the stacking tab
//! (after the Sequator manual).

pub fn note(name: &str) -> &'static str {
    match name {
        "Star images" => "Star shots with the same focal length, aperture and exposure; they are stacked onto the base image. RAW is best; TIFF, FITS or XISF only if the data is still linear.\n\nUnify exposure: evens out the brightness of frames taken under different conditions (e.g. other nights), which otherwise leave rectangular borders.\n\nQuick preview stacks up to 10 frames at low resolution and saves nothing.",
        "Base image" => "The frame the others are aligned and stacked onto. If none is set, the middle star image (by file name) is used.\n\nClick a star image and press “Use as base” to choose one.",
        "Noise images" => "Dark frames: lens cap on, same ISO and exposure time as the star images, taken right before and after them (noise changes with temperature). Their median is subtracted, and the hot pixels found in them are replaced.",
        "Bias images" => "Bias frames: shortest exposure with the lens cap on, same ISO: the sensor's offset. Subtracted from the flats when there are no flat darks, and from the star images when there are no noise images.",
        "Vignetting images" => "Flat frames: an even, white subject (paper or cloth over the lens against a uniform light, a white wall), same focal length and aperture, properly exposed. They correct the light fall-off towards the corners (and dust shadows).\n\nHomogenize vignetting averages and blurs the four quadrants, for uneven flats. Not suitable if there are dust shadows.",
        "Flat-dark images" => "Dark frames for the flats: same ISO and exposure time as the vignetting images, lens cap on. Subtracted from the flats (else the bias is).",
        "Foreground image" => "A separate shot of the ground from the same tripod position – at blue hour, a long exposure or low ISO – used below the sky region instead of the stacked foreground (which is noisy or dark at night). Needs a sky region. Not aligned: the ground stays in place.\n\nMatch brightness scales it to the stacked ground, so it fits the night; Exposure (EV) brightens or darkens it from there (+1 EV = twice as bright).",
        "Output" => "TIFF (.tif): 16-bit, as shown. JPEG (.jpg): 8-bit. FITS (.fits) or XISF (.xisf): 32-bit float, linear: only the brightness gain is applied, no tone curve, nothing clipped – the most room for processing in Siril, PixInsight etc.\nAlign only writes the aligned frames into an “aligned” folder next to the output (FITS / XISF if the output is, else TIFF).",
        "Composition" => "Align stars:\n  Accumulation – average of the aligned frames.\n  Select best pixels – sigma-clipped average (see Sigma clipping).\n  Freeze ground – the sky is aligned, the foreground is not, so both stay sharp. Needs a sky region and a fixed tripod.\n  Align only – writes the aligned frames as linear images.\nSelective: sigma clipping also in the sky (removes planes, meteors).\nModel: homography follows the perspective of wide-angle shots; similarity only shifts, turns, scales.\nInterpolation: Lanczos is sharper, bilinear a little smoother and faster.\n\nStar trails: no alignment; the sky gets the brightest value, the foreground the average.\n  Trail fade – fade in (Sequator's motion effect: the trails start faint), fade out, or both ends (a comet-like taper).\n  Fill gaps – the short breaks between frames (the camera's interval) are filled along the measured star motion, so the trails are continuous instead of dashed.",
        "Sky region" => "Marks the sky: stars are detected there, Freeze ground, Star trails and the foreground image treat the rest as foreground.\n  Boundary line – drag a line along the horizon.\n  Gradient – the line plus a transition zone below it.\n  Irregular mask – paint the sky (left button), erase (right); mouse wheel or the slider = brush size.\n  Automatic – detected in the base image at the start of each run.\nDetect sky paints a first mask from brightness and stars (approximate: check it and correct with the brush). Mask feather softens the mask's edge, inside the sky only, so the foreground gets none of the sky. Auxiliary highlight brightens the view; Show detected stars marks the stars the last run found in the shown frame.",
        "Sigma clipping" => "Select best pixels, Freeze ground and the star-trails foreground drop values further than low / high × σ from the centre, a few times over. Lower values remove more (planes, satellites, hot pixels) but leave more noise; 2–3 is usual. Needs 4 or more frames, works best with 10+.",
        "Frame rejection" => "Every star image is measured: stars found, star size (FWHM), elongation, sky level, brightness of its brightest stars compared with the base image (light), alignment error. Automatic rejection leaves out frames clearly worse than the typical one: dimmer stars (clouds, haze, dew), few stars, blurred or elongated stars (focus, wind, bumped tripod). The base image is never rejected.\nSee the Frame report after Preview or Start: a chart of every measure per frame (÷ its median, so outliers stand out; red = left out) above the table, and the log of every step. Click a frame in either to exclude or include it.",
        "RAW conversion" => "RAW files are decoded without tone curve, as stacking needs, by the built-in decoder (or LibRaw's dcraw_emu / dcraw if chosen). White balance: the camera's, the image average, or daylight. Demosaicing applies to dcraw_emu and dcraw: AHD (default); DHT and AAHD often give less colour noise at high ISO; DCB, PPG, VNG, bilinear. Half size skips demosaicing: 4× fewer pixels, much faster, less memory. The quick preview always uses half size.",
        "Reduce dynamic noises" => "Finds hot pixels without noise images (single pixels much brighter than all their neighbours) and replaces them. No detection is perfect.",
        "Reduce distor. effects" => "Lens distortion and refraction move the stars differently across the frame, which leaves short trails in the corners of long sequences.\n  Lens – one radial distortion for all frames (simple lenses, telescopes).\n  Complex – also a smooth correction per frame, learned from the stars (ultra wide-angle, refraction near the horizon). Needs many stars.\n  Auto – Complex when enough stars match, else Lens.\nThe Frame report's align column shows the remaining error.",
        "Merge 4 pixels" => "Adds up 2×2 neighbouring pixels: brighter and less noisy, but half the width and height.",
        "Time-lapse" => "Stacks every N consecutive frames into one low-noise frame: K star images give K − N + 1 frames, numbered 0001… in a folder named after the output (e.g. stacked_timelapse). Each is aligned to its middle frame, so the sky still moves; all get the same brightness (no flicker). Quick preview shows the first one.",
        "Auto brightness" => "Puts the sky background at a pleasant level without letting more than the brightest 0.1 % of the sky clip. Off: the brightness of a single frame.",
        "Background extraction" => "Subtracts a smooth model of the sky background (gradients from light pollution, moonlight, vignetting), as Siril's background extraction does; the sky keeps its average level. Degree 1 = a plain gradient, higher = more curved.",
        "Color calibration" => "Basic colour calibration without plate solving: the sky background becomes neutral grey and the average star white.",
        "Reduce colour noise" => "Smooths only the colour (not the brightness): removes the coloured speckles of high-ISO shots. Stronger = smoother, but small stars lose some colour.",
        "Tone" => "How the linear stack becomes a viewable image: sRGB curve (as a camera does), Stretch (black point, midtones, white point – automatic like Siril's autostretch, or by hand), or Linear (no curve, for further processing). FITS / XISF are always saved linear.",
        "Linear" => "Linear (for further processing): everything here off, no brightness change, no tone curve, output as 32-bit FITS – what Siril, PixInsight etc. expect. Use their background extraction, colour calibration and stretch afterwards.",
        "High dynamic range" => "Compresses the highlights instead of clipping them (bright stars, lights); with Auto brightness the dark parts are lifted more.",
        "Reduce light pollution" => "Removes a model of the sky background, leaving an even, neutral sky. Correct the vignetting first (vignetting images), or it gets worse.\n  Deep sky – a smooth polynomial; Degree 1 = a plain gradient, 2–4 = more curved glows.\n  Uneven – follows the glow tile by tile (wide-angle, domes of city light); fewer tiles = smoother. Very large structures (the Milky Way) may be reduced with many tiles.\nI.A.A. (Uneven + sky region): also corrects foreground areas with the colour of the glow.\nShow removed background shows the model in the Result view. Don't overdo it.",
        "Enhance star light" => "Lifts the stars above their surroundings in the sky region; the background and its noise are left alone.",
        "Color space" => "Colours of the TIFF / JPEG output: sRGB (default) or Adobe RGB (wider gamut; no ICC profile is embedded – assign Adobe RGB in your editor). FITS / XISF are always linear sRGB.",
        _ => "",
    }
}
